WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:02.640
Have you ever wondered

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.560
if there are more natural ways
to lower your high blood pressure,

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.520
guard against Alzheimer's,
lose weight, and feel better?

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.720
Well, it turns out there is!

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.200
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.880
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.640
Today, we look at the effects
of animals on our health

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:23.600
and we start with the question,

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:29.680
do pet parents like me
(or pawrents) live longer?

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.680
Intense social connectivity
is a Blue Zone attribute

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.600
scrutinized for
its potential role

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.200
in supporting longevity.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.400
Does slobbery
social contact count?

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.160
More than two-thirds
of U.S. households

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.160
include a pet (including mine!).

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.640
In an "awwww"-inspiring paper

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.040
published in the prestigious
journal Science entitled

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:50.880
"Oxytocin-gaze positive loop

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.600
and the coevolution
of human-dog bonds,"

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.480
researchers found that petting
or looking into the eyes

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.600
of a canine companion
leads to oxytocin release

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.920
in the brains of both
the humans and the dogs,

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.040
the same "love hormone" that
bonds breastfeeding mothers

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.560
to their infants.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.480
I was reading about
the potential mechanisms

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:10.400
by which having
an animal companion

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.480
might improve survival
after a heart attack

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.920
when I ran across a passage

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.720
about a "profound"
cardiovascular response

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.800
when petting dogs or horses.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:20.560
"This response
usually takes the form

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.000
of a significant reduction

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.320
in the heart rate
and blood pressure."

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.160
I could totally see that.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.600
But then the next sentence
made me do a double take:

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.360
"Unfortunately,
we have no information

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:32.960
about
the physiological responses

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.520
of the person
doing the petting."

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.760
Oh, so they were talking
about the heart rate

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.560
and blood pressure
of the animals!

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.120
To my surprise,
studies of the effects

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.440
of companion animals
on human health have,

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.120
as one review put it,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.720
produced a "mishmash
of conflicting results."

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.320
But a meta-analysis of studies

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.320
involving more than
three million people

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.080
followed for an average
of a decade

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.920
found that
canine companionship,

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:58.720
for example,

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.880
was associated with
a 24 percent reduction

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.200
in the risk
of premature death.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.080
The authors suggested
that enhanced survival

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.400
may be a result
of increased physical activity

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.080
from walking the dog.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.240
That could also help explain
why pet ownership

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.520
has been associated
with a lower risk of frailty.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:18.720
Of course, this too
could be reverse causation,

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.160
where people
who are already active

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.120
are more likely to get a dog
in the first place,

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.320
but the greater issue
may be confounding factors.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.320
Pet owners tend to be
younger, wealthier,

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:33.040
more educated,
and more married.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:35.040
Sadly, when these types
of elements

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.400
are taken into account,

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.000
the significant mortality risk
reduction disappears.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.440
The same appears to be true
for pet ownership in general.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:44.800
The higher survival rate

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.880
becomes
statistically insignificant

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:50.240
once confounding factors,
like socioeconomic factors,

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.320
are taken into account.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.880
When it comes specifically
to cats,

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.000
confounding can work
in the opposite direction.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.680
Given evidence
that social support

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.000
may help with survival
after a heart attack,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.600
a number of studies
have been undertaken

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.760
to see if pet ownership
can help.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.360
One study found
that cat owners

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.560
had a significantly
greater risk of death.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.080
This may be
because they appear

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.640
to not only be more likely
to be sedentary

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.400
than people with dogs,
who may be going on more walks,

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.880
but cat owners
may also be more sedentary

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.400
than people
without any pets at all.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.880
Given the pitfalls
of trying to impute

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.120
cause-and-effect
from observational studies,

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.840
I was so excited to see
a randomized controlled trial

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.840
that actually put pet ownership
to the test.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.720
Don't get your hopes
up too much, though.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:39.520
The title was,

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:42.880
"Effect of Pet Insects
on the Psychological Health

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.120
of Community-Dwelling
Elderly People:

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.240
A Single-Blinded, Randomized,
Controlled Trial."

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.000
The "pets" were crickets.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:53.760
But compared to
the control group,

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.000
older individuals
(average age 72)

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.400
randomized to take care
of crickets

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:00.800
experienced a significantly
greater improvement

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.960
in depression,
cognitive status,

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:04.560
and mental quality of life.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.760
So, maybe there's hope
for Fido yet.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.160
Most studies on pets
and human health

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:12.000
are snapshot-in-time studies

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.720
where it's impossible to know
which came first,

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.200
but longitudinal studies
over time have shown

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.760
that dog walkers
are indeed more likely

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.200
to achieve
recommended activity levels.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.600
Sadly, only a minority--

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.400
only about a third--
of older dog owners

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.920
were found to even walk
their dogs three times a week.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.440
To maximize the benefits
for pooch and person alike,

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:36.560
here's wise advice

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:40.400
from a medical journal article
published in 1925:

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.040
"The best prescription
to be written for a walk

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.000
is to take a dog..."

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:46.720
Finally today,

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:48.960
we look at how sharing your
home with a cat or dog

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:52.880
may decrease the risk of
infectious diseases in children—

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.440
including ear infections, respiratory
illnesses, and gastroenteritis.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.280
To my surprise,

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:03.280
“studies of the effects of pets on human
health and well-being have [evidently]

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.320
produced a mishmash
of conflicting results.”

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:09.920
Some studies show pets
lower your blood pressure—

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:13.760
in fact, in some cases,
more than even drugs do.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:19.280
But, other studies found no effect,
or even that pet owners

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.000
have higher blood pressure.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.000
Does having a pet increase
your survival after a heart attack,

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.680
or decrease your survival
after a heart attack?

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.640
One area where there’s a bit more
consistency is children’s health.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:38.640
The presence of furry pets in the home
appears to cut the odds

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:40.880
of acute respiratory illnesses in half,

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.760
and may even decrease the risk
of getting the common cold.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:48.000
But, which pets work better?

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:55.360
Cats like my Charlotte, Emily,
and Ralph, or dogs like my Lilly?

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:59.360
Published recently in the official journal
of the American Academy of Pediatrics:

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:02.480
“Respiratory Tract Illnesses
During the First Year of Life:

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.840
Effect of Dog and Cat Contacts.”

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:09.040
“[T]he first study that has
evaluated the significance

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:12.960
of pet contacts during childhood
for the development of respiratory tract

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:16.640
[symptoms and] infections,”
including ear infections.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.680
They found “dog and cat contacts during
early infancy may be associated with

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:24.720
less [illness] in general…and…
may have a protective effect

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.400
on respiratory tract
symptoms and infections.”

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:31.200
But as to which is better,

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.040
“In comparisons between
cat and dog contacts,

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:39.120
dog contacts showed
a more significant protective role

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.160
on respiratory infectious disease…”

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:44.880
“[C]hildren having a dog at home
were significantly healthier,

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.440
had less frequent
[ear infections],

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.520
and tended to need fewer courses
of antibiotics during the study period

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:54.800
than children without dog contacts.”

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.880
“Cat ownership seemed to also
have an overall protective effect,

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:04.160
although weaker than dog ownership,
on the infectious health of infants.”

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:08.000
Though, when it comes to protecting
children from tummy aches,

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.600
both cats and dogs appeared
equally effective in reducing

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:15.680
the risks of gastroenteritis.

